WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.361
Picture the restaurant on a busy Saturday.

2
00:00:05.361 --> 00:00:07.926
Most dishes leave the kitchen in minutes.

3
00:00:07.926 --> 00:00:12.242
But now and then, a table waits half an hour, and nobody can say why.

4
00:00:12.242 --> 00:00:13.619
The cooks aren't slow.

5
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:18.018
Then someone notices that every few minutes, the whole kitchen stops.

6
00:00:18.018 --> 00:00:21.564
The clean plates have run out, and everyone helps wash up.

7
00:00:21.564 --> 00:00:26.516
Every step of every dish uses plates, and some steps use far more than they need.

8
00:00:26.516 --> 00:00:28.228
Nobody ordered extra plates.

9
00:00:28.228 --> 00:00:29.940
They just keep disappearing.

10
00:00:30.867 --> 00:00:33.217
Our report step has the same shape.

11
00:00:33.217 --> 00:00:34.627
Most calls are quick.

12
00:00:34.627 --> 00:00:38.052
So here's the question that's easy to be shy about.

13
00:00:38.052 --> 00:00:41.207
If the average is fine, isn't the program fine?

14
00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:42.953
Why would anyone complain?

15
00:00:46.533 --> 00:00:50.432
Here's the code under suspicion, the report step from lesson 6.

16
00:00:50.432 --> 00:00:58.601
It checks the two-letter language with a regex, counts the words with split, and formats a line for a debug log that's switched off.

17
00:00:58.601 --> 00:01:03.428
The lesson's demo runs it on four threads for 3 seconds, and times every call.

18
00:01:04.333 --> 00:01:09.600
Now picture every call standing in one line, from fastest to slowest.

19
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.890
p50 is the call in the middle.

20
00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:15.401
p99 is the call that 99 percent of calls beat.

21
00:01:15.401 --> 00:01:19.600
And at p99.99, only one call in ten thousand is slower.

22
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.951
Those rare slow calls at the far end are called the tail.

23
00:01:24.133 --> 00:01:27.535
Half the calls take under 1.5 microseconds.

24
00:01:27.535 --> 00:01:30.937
How much slower is the one in ten thousand?

25
00:01:30.937 --> 00:01:31.412
Twice?

26
00:01:31.412 --> 00:01:32.203
Ten times?

27
00:01:35.467 --> 00:01:36.753
Over 36 milliseconds.

28
00:01:36.753 --> 00:01:38.590
More than 20,000 times longer.

29
00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:43.244
If the middle call took one minute, that one would take more than two weeks.

30
00:01:43.244 --> 00:01:47.470
So the average, and even the middle call, say nothing about the tail.

31
00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:50.348
And the tail is where the complaints come from.

32
00:01:50.533 --> 00:01:53.500
Now look at the end of the line.

33
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:57.950
The JVM collected garbage 14 times in 3 seconds.

34
00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:07.961
JFR's gc-pauses view, on the same recording, agrees: 14 pauses, 236 milliseconds in total, the longest 53.2.

35
00:02:08.867 --> 00:02:12.159
That's our first suspect, and the kitchen explains it.

36
00:02:12.159 --> 00:02:16.183
Every object a call creates is a clean plate taken from the shelf.

37
00:02:16.183 --> 00:02:20.390
The shelf is the young part of the heap, where new objects start out.

38
00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:26.243
When it's empty, the garbage collector stops every thread while it clears the dead objects away.

39
00:02:26.243 --> 00:02:27.951
The whole kitchen washes up.

40
00:02:28.133 --> 00:02:32.297
A call that's running when the pause starts has to wait for it to end.

41
00:02:32.297 --> 00:02:34.735
That's the table that waits half an hour.

42
00:02:34.735 --> 00:02:42.169
So the slowest calls are probably the ones that met a pause, and the pauses come often because the code takes so many plates.

43
00:02:42.333 --> 00:02:44.422
Here the picture breaks a little.

44
00:02:44.422 --> 00:02:51.573
G1, the JVM's default collector, does some of its work while the program runs, like a porter washing in a corner.

45
00:02:51.573 --> 00:02:55.687
But the young collections, the frequent ones here, stop everyone.

46
00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:57.843
One honest caution.

47
00:02:57.843 --> 00:03:05.168
This machine shared its four cores with more than 30 busy programs, so some slow calls simply waited for a core.

48
00:03:05.168 --> 00:03:08.439
Don't blame the longest call of a run on anything.

49
00:03:08.439 --> 00:03:11.709
The percentiles and the GC count are the evidence.

50
00:03:11.867 --> 00:03:12.757
So, rule one.

51
00:03:12.757 --> 00:03:17.208
A good median can hide calls that wait for the garbage collector.

52
00:03:17.208 --> 00:03:19.673
Measure the tail, and count the GCs.

53
00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:23.590
Next: which lines take the plates?

54
00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:33.440
JFR samples allocations with a stack trace, and the lesson groups the samples by the first line in our own code.

55
00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:37.925
Line 21, the debug line nobody reads: 49.1 percent.

56
00:03:37.925 --> 00:03:42.146
Line 17, the regex compiled on every call: 32.3.

57
00:03:42.146 --> 00:03:43.993
Line 19, split: 18.5.

58
00:03:44.133 --> 00:03:46.779
It's tempting to fix all three at once.

59
00:03:46.779 --> 00:03:47.185
Don't.

60
00:03:47.185 --> 00:03:49.763
Then you never learn which one helped.

61
00:03:49.763 --> 00:03:57.088
Round 1 removes the pure waste: the regex is compiled once, and the log line is built only when debug is on.

62
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.117
Same harness, same busy machine.

63
00:04:01.117 --> 00:04:09.200
3.5 times the calls per second, and the p99.99 down from 36.7 milliseconds to 12.4.

64
00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:13.096
But the GC count went up, from 14 to 15.

65
00:04:13.096 --> 00:04:14.752
Did the fix fail?

66
00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:18.039
No.

67
00:04:18.039 --> 00:04:23.365
The faster code made 3.5 times as many calls in the same 3 seconds.

68
00:04:23.365 --> 00:04:29.487
Count per call: about 21 collections per million calls before, about 6.5 now.

69
00:04:29.487 --> 00:04:33.144
A busier kitchen, and fewer wash-ups per dish.

70
00:04:33.333 --> 00:04:37.559
Then profile again, because the picture has changed.

71
00:04:37.559 --> 00:04:41.459
split was about a quarter of the problem before.

72
00:04:41.459 --> 00:04:43.247
Now it's 91.5 percent.

73
00:04:43.247 --> 00:04:48.203
Round 2 counts where words start, without building any words.

74
00:04:48.203 --> 00:04:54.785
The p99.99 falls to 51 microseconds, and the GCs to about 0.65 per million calls.

75
00:04:55.667 --> 00:04:58.185
Last, prove it to the byte.

76
00:04:58.185 --> 00:05:07.046
JMH, the benchmark tool from lesson 3, reports bytes per call: 2,464 before, then 832, then 56.

77
00:05:07.046 --> 00:05:10.684
On this busy machine the times wobbled.

78
00:05:10.684 --> 00:05:12.269
The bytes didn't.

79
00:05:12.467 --> 00:05:14.644
What's left is a 56 byte matcher.

80
00:05:14.644 --> 00:05:17.878
The step's budget is 300 bytes, so we stop there.

81
00:05:17.878 --> 00:05:23.025
And a test checks that budget on every build, so the plates stay on the shelf.

82
00:05:23.933 --> 00:05:27.065
So, if the average is fine, is the program fine?

83
00:05:27.065 --> 00:05:29.609
Not when the tail meets the washing up.

84
00:05:29.609 --> 00:05:32.609
Start from the slowest calls and the GC count.

85
00:05:32.609 --> 00:05:34.958
Find the line that takes the plates.

86
00:05:34.958 --> 00:05:36.980
Fix one thing, then look again.

87
00:05:37.133 --> 00:05:37.929
Quick check.

88
00:05:37.929 --> 00:05:42.238
In round 1, you hoist the regex but forget to guard the log line.

89
00:05:42.238 --> 00:05:44.492
Which line leads the next profile?

90
00:05:47.733 --> 00:05:54.588
Line 21, the String.format: about 810 bytes per call, now the biggest cost left.
